#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092059 :question/id #uuid "bc48e845-b2a7-5e30-9c73-b7d842f4090c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5383" :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $\\left( 6,\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\right).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\tx &= r\\cos\\theta\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 6 \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 6 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2}\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 3\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\ty &= r\\sin\\theta\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 6 \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 6 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 3\\sqrt{3}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(3, 3\\sqrt{3}\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092060 :question/id #uuid "3454059c-9847-5bb2-9732-e33885afa462" :question/math-academy-id "e-5384" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $2,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $135^{\\circ}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ where\n\n$$\nr=2, \\quad \\theta=135^{\\circ}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\tx &= r\\cos\\theta\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &=2 \\cos\\left( 135^{\\circ} \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 2 \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= -\\sqrt{2}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\ty &= r\\sin\\theta\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 2 \\sin\\left(135^{\\circ}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 2 \\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= \\sqrt{2}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(  -\\sqrt{2},  \\sqrt{2}\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092061 :question/id #uuid "63b5b1e6-1885-554c-973c-bd78555f44ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-5385" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $\\left(r, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6} \\right)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $\\left(5r,\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(3,-\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\tr &= \\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\\\\[3pt]\n\t\t &=\\sqrt{3^2+(\\sqrt{3})^2}\\\\[3pt]\n\t\t &= \\sqrt{12} \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t &= 2\\sqrt{3}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(5(2\\sqrt{3}),\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\left(10\\sqrt{3},\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\tx &= r\\cos\\theta\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t&=10\\sqrt{3} \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t&= 10\\sqrt{3} \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t&= -15\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\ty &= r\\sin\\theta\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t&=10\\sqrt{3} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t&= 10\\sqrt{3} \\cdot\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t&=5\\sqrt{3}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $\\left( -15,  5\\sqrt{3}\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092112 :question/id #uuid "f0c5bd75-dd47-5028-8eff-38c87e7411bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-7116" :question/problem "The diagram below shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $\\left(3,\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ and $m \\angle AOB=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$\n\n![](images/a0/a0d06318ba26eee754bc3581f5a0f1c126eb0723f8aea2cac0fad075ee56e7f9.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $\\left(3,\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right),$ we must have $r=3.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $3,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $3.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ the $\\theta$-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n\\theta_B = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $\\left(3,\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\tx &= r\\cos\\theta\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &=3\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &=3\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &=-\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\ty &= r\\sin\\theta\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 3\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 3\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t &=\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092597 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83908dd0-d198-52b3-8826-afa1429dbb50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089754} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae2cd5b-f109-5dbb-bf75-ab22f3567103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089755} {:answer/id #uuid "0c3da346-d7d9-5148-b044-6261a6a7a773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089756} {:answer/id #uuid "46f644a7-41c7-575e-90ea-b0bdf7907385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089757} {:answer/id #uuid "4e49a3e0-69dd-5cc5-8e4e-d960cd9640b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089758}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1c74ba52-6920-5ddb-99ba-e1cd12c20908" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089753}] :question/id #uuid "a5be0c71-ace5-571a-88cf-4ce8508ffc58" :question/math-academy-id "q-17133" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $1$, and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{5π}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092703 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdf20096-bc79-5589-a630-1641f8fe0ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{15}, 2\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090419} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c9ff15-216c-5b7c-bf56-2cfa31cb97af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{15}, 2\\sqrt{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090420} {:answer/id #uuid "5d034e1a-b724-530e-affc-63bac67f2a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{5}, 2\\sqrt{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090421} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca499d2-67e9-5fc6-a4c5-5208506dc65c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{5}, \\sqrt{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090422} {:answer/id #uuid "7f0b692a-5390-54a9-a6f7-739b88f06ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{15}, 4\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090423}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4a4d92fd-ac4b-5f7a-9c34-e0bfcd92fac9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090418}] :question/id #uuid "0ebc9d27-9c03-5dba-a9e8-e61d4df48f75" :question/math-academy-id "q-40803" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r, \\frac{π}{4})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r, \\frac{π}{3})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(\\sqrt{10}, \\sqrt{10})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0d74418-5a15-53d0-9b0f-29a8ffb06a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090425} {:answer/id #uuid "decad71a-b4e9-5d7b-9199-8f0aee31dcd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090426} {:answer/id #uuid "e90c9538-684f-5cce-a8ea-7f6831fa103a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090427} {:answer/id #uuid "512c0a28-3bdb-5347-b37e-b43ef2062001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090428} {:answer/id #uuid "55cd377c-4643-5da3-a323-25622bb33cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090429}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3c7a661a-09c6-5373-b0d4-2134575fd07d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090424}] :question/id #uuid "3dd4897a-0f39-5ff6-8f3b-6202a2c681b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-40806" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(2, θ)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(1, θ - \\frac{π}{4})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d121349d-31c9-5bac-922f-a3d7427c2e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{2}, 4\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090431} {:answer/id #uuid "d94ac2cb-77dc-5c8e-8b60-7ebe1cd327a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4, 2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090432} {:answer/id #uuid "00cc892a-647a-5731-8038-c03a6486c06e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4, 2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090433} {:answer/id #uuid "351d03cf-8046-5fde-9515-8c70b37ccacf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{2}, 4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090434} {:answer/id #uuid "6a204728-2a70-5b4e-a407-acd285e5b284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4, 4\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090435}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ab58c44e-78a4-588e-a3bb-863a1a4a410d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921860454