:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254387} :answer-field/id #uuid "5736a212-726e-5d37-950e-cb5984448244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cffd31fb-6e24-58d9-bedb-a4c483fcb431" :question/math-academy-id "q-120311" :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(4,{225}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡({225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-2\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48fc86a1-f2ca-5fb6-98bb-2ba50587b6e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254297} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2e5dea-eb32-5ef7-b053-292457b74c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254647} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e86342-c856-59b9-acc7-8c16b2779f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255156} {:answer/id #uuid "bceb10bc-923e-5dff-8dfb-386a60cdb562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6\\sqrt{2},6\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255360} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3e4f69-01dd-5d42-b6e1-f9b19a563b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48fc86a1-f2ca-5fb6-98bb-2ba50587b6e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254297} :answer-field/id #uuid "af20ccec-9741-587e-8b2c-0fb701159ee6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3fe83e25-a491-5f7c-b624-e2aecbbcfc65" :question/math-academy-id "q-120328" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $6,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${315}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=6,\\,\\theta ={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =6\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a155516-d839-587a-bed9-42b9a2e072c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6\\sqrt{3},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253747} {:answer/id #uuid "1129da34-d7d1-5e9f-8843-f5403e6bf93a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253820} {:answer/id #uuid "12c049a4-b3f5-57ad-8839-06ff45915613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253833} {:answer/id #uuid "4009cc1a-951f-501b-bdc2-6e69fb5a6357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254222} {:answer/id #uuid "a9834412-7636-54d2-8673-12769dbe9d79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a155516-d839-587a-bed9-42b9a2e072c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6\\sqrt{3},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253747} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4d936aa-147e-55d6-93f2-fbed67c48196" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b0e5e2dd-de81-57dc-8848-06ae6eac4cea" :question/math-academy-id "q-120455" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $({r}^{2},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-3,\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n((\\sqrt{12})^{2},\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=(12,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =12\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =12\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(6\\sqrt{3},-6).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256409 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a02ee7-0c84-56f1-bbf0-ebb90394da60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253859} {:answer/id #uuid "39bbabdf-ded0-54de-9bce-39f8a04d806d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254176} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9d34e5-3a45-5295-96d7-05130d5b7bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254659} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7d44b2-7e30-57fa-9135-30c7aad08a99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254758} {:answer/id #uuid "c869f985-2a3d-5a8b-ba06-6ea45dcdacab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39bbabdf-ded0-54de-9bce-39f8a04d806d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254176} :answer-field/id #uuid "8600f656-d47f-52c4-96f7-5d633c8eeb59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6d3f790c-6d1a-5380-94bb-56056dda53c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-120468" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-\\sqrt{3},-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-1}{-\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1/\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(4,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=(4,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(0,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5da02127-9e0f-517d-b833-10a14ea966be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,3\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254500} {:answer/id #uuid "872e2a4f-200b-5af7-8500-67bf6abe3f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254867} {:answer/id #uuid "aa36bbb1-b88c-5b72-bb8b-94823eb95074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255175} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f185a7-e803-56ef-9e38-6ce29b1a5f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\sqrt{2},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255586} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f56308-c9ed-5d20-99cf-9b1ba40c9435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},3\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5da02127-9e0f-517d-b833-10a14ea966be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,3\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254500} :answer-field/id #uuid "562137bc-c0ee-5964-b8f9-1d239f29ef6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ec6a7f4f-9f9a-5a0c-bdce-ef6083c2204a" :question/math-academy-id "q-120593" :question/problem "![](images/48/48517c5d3fa340bed1cc7b5493f0099a72afa79d9e0690b79695afe018c8a05c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(6,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(6,\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we must have $r=6.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $6,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $6.$ \n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(6,\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-3,3\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b0831bf-55aa-510a-918a-90cd99cdc701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254315} {:answer/id #uuid "54a4837d-2402-5b85-885b-20b64294842f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254408} {:answer/id #uuid "7962a6c2-849e-5bfd-90f7-16eb630fa284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254745} {:answer/id #uuid "87c149ae-d2b4-5a7d-8b6f-ca93c28e9563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\sqrt{3},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254874} {:answer/id #uuid "f870bd85-6f4b-5e70-909a-8712db2320fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f870bd85-6f4b-5e70-909a-8712db2320fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255845} :answer-field/id #uuid "da62c4ed-dbe1-5f2f-b50d-04b6985ed206" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0eb03380-bb2e-5373-833c-3b08aac5da8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-120595" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5ff0219f3f76441dbffc770ce0b8f2481cca8c46b8aa832ab7893d472f3026ae.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),$ we must have $r=4.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4,\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-2,2\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0484ad77-2f99-5c81-a6cb-a1158be44d88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253704} {:answer/id #uuid "15246ab6-3ecb-5633-9b4e-b73f8ed07f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{2},-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253853} {:answer/id #uuid "31095a2e-0934-55af-abe5-a2f05014f85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254098} {:answer/id #uuid "3f307f37-58c4-56a4-a6dd-e9b99b686e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254213} {:answer/id #uuid "e1629e74-354a-56e5-bbe5-bfd524f9a56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31095a2e-0934-55af-abe5-a2f05014f85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254098} :answer-field/id #uuid "e62cfd60-2bdd-5ae1-8632-0391624de18c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4cadc848-bed5-5538-a431-ac83515fffa4" :question/math-academy-id "q-120597" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd38e4bed4f11f07f2c6901a74c7543655f5ff8a95b8b049e54ff4ff4dd409ee.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},{150}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={165}^{∘},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4\\sqrt{2},{150}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=4\\sqrt{2}.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4\\sqrt{2},$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4\\sqrt{2}.$  \n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${150}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={150}^{∘}+{165}^{∘}={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},{315}^{∘})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(4,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9724a5f6-c9f4-536a-a17b-5425407b9c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255002} {:answer/id #uuid "ea54590c-b0df-5003-9417-4c09af7236b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255723} {:answer/id #uuid "eb87a981-771e-5056-8d37-a93f3b1e2bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255730} {:answer/id #uuid "feab8e4b-ee4c-5116-8ef8-f1f48cf2bd2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255909} {:answer/id #uuid "ff088a24-bea8-531d-8f92-d5dff5c8b72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea54590c-b0df-5003-9417-4c09af7236b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255723} :answer-field/id #uuid "47c6223c-72f8-56bd-b93f-b161db175405" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1f77f386-bd94-517d-b58d-73799834bcb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-120598" :question/problem "![](images/47/475bda1ee758774ec9b07ec12a0ed92e56c4b571fd39f6e1ecca439278106ea6.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(2,{240}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={195}^{∘},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,{240}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=2.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $2,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $2.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${240}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={240}^{∘}-{195}^{∘}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(2,{45}^{∘})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284678 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "753543d7-b063-5cf6-b128-a80a7409bfa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282693} {:answer/id #uuid "76d18bf0-adc0-56ad-a666-eb6830554a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282705} {:answer/id #uuid "9e0cfff2-f165-5112-84b4-84ef6d0d16d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283067} {:answer/id #uuid "b46da797-d4b1-5e93-b5d2-956b71f67813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283252} {:answer/id #uuid "e9302e03-d1d5-5540-a03c-b0e2ce178144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b46da797-d4b1-5e93-b5d2-956b71f67813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283252} :answer-field/id #uuid "24c6099d-d0ba-56c6-9aa9-eadf358e9b15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f8753c4e-3481-5eda-bb21-d60f3fd06725" :question/math-academy-id "q-17124" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $\\frac{1}{3},$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284679 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6579eacc-068b-5cac-8c3b-3d0005500a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282560} {:answer/id #uuid "77f0a4c2-79c0-54cd-8fc3-9692d5df1551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282714} {:answer/id #uuid "88c83019-e455-582f-ae36-d2343335742f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282869} {:answer/id #uuid "9919d5ba-4b95-5580-84fc-627c631faff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283018} {:answer/id #uuid "baa4a7a7-5652-5d2b-84aa-4fd087f99b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "baa4a7a7-5652-5d2b-84aa-4fd087f99b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283299} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fa5c78f-ca4a-5dae-af0e-6861f5fb66c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6312ff71-6d99-5de0-8860-37b893c00f52" :question/math-academy-id "q-17126" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $2,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=2,\\,\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ x & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ y & =2\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "359a8b1a-2e90-5221-b6b6-45c8e9ae12e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282165} {:answer/id #uuid "5b3df71b-aee2-5f35-9b12-53d884eac1df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282460} {:answer/id #uuid "65df5433-d222-576b-bdee-9211bd3a1f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282563} {:answer/id #uuid "6d834bc3-a336-5730-9fdf-30a9b3c13603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282627} {:answer/id #uuid "fba3c6d5-e0fb-5390-90be-e8ccc191e99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65df5433-d222-576b-bdee-9211bd3a1f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282563} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0606b74-307e-5421-b86b-2c8a159b55ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "648c4b24-94b5-5a05-aa36-37a2f263b7f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-17128" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $2,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=2,\\,\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2(-1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(0,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f7d1dc9-08a0-5c97-af85-8958c4f26edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281972} {:answer/id #uuid "630c3a59-ef83-541f-b9f3-e86f0db79abb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282540} {:answer/id #uuid "6d760ce6-7b53-5b17-95f0-2a701da851f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282625} {:answer/id #uuid "6fda8237-6723-59bd-aa75-aa04c8fefcec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282646} {:answer/id #uuid "8297ba5c-921d-5ae0-bd90-2ee58b877b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fda8237-6723-59bd-aa75-aa04c8fefcec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282646} :answer-field/id #uuid "684d27ef-fb36-5be2-9f3b-9d537878d018" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0cbc4e4b-2d27-5640-8947-f02e086abba5" :question/math-academy-id "q-17134" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $4,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=4,\\,\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\ x & =4\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\ y & =4\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\ y & =-2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-2,-2\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284682 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0068e4a5-cb28-5519-9eed-397f1fd495ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281690} {:answer/id #uuid "24c5f2d9-5d73-5dfb-8877-22831e9dfcc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282010} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc39e53-d504-5253-a096-83f4724ee131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282113} {:answer/id #uuid "62a281e2-ead9-5741-8c03-34eb83922464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282534} {:answer/id #uuid "eba4daee-0b6f-57bd-8eb5-6f8a3b202d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fc39e53-d504-5253-a096-83f4724ee131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282113} :answer-field/id #uuid "f111caec-f6e3-5cc4-a5b9-929e5f8a60b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dbe89916-9884-50d0-99bb-1478386ff8b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-17137" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $7,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=7,\\,\\theta =0.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ x & =7\\cdot 1 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ y & =7\\cdot 0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(7,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c01297c0-4360-5196-b378-cd6d24b00a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c01297c0-4360-5196-b378-cd6d24b00a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293681} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a18b0e0-b9aa-52e0-98f1-977eb7e19dc9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294253} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "468ac213-e7f6-5724-9735-7787b9bbd432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "468ac213-e7f6-5724-9735-7787b9bbd432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293221} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cc6c722-0281-52d1-9044-934e70f285b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eb68afad-979d-5067-bbdf-a0270d1ebbf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-276511" :question/problem "If the point $P$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ then its Cartesian coordinates are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\ x & =2\\cdot 0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\ y & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(0,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8b2ae77-8b62-5d95-92cd-30b386d31fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8b2ae77-8b62-5d95-92cd-30b386d31fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293653} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fabd070-935d-5a2e-a288-75e37fb6b529" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294504} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d50cbd5a-22d6-5311-8256-9d985b42f1cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d50cbd5a-22d6-5311-8256-9d985b42f1cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293758} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5b8ae25-12a1-5948-afc5-b6d6a4c132c3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "41b67774-fd1a-58ed-b47c-db421e0a8ae7" :question/math-academy-id "q-276514" :question/problem "If the point $P$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}),$ then its Cartesian coordinates are\n\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-2\\sqrt{3},-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bec1215c-a928-5e0d-bdca-f0ee1a9eeb09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bec1215c-a928-5e0d-bdca-f0ee1a9eeb09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 1