swer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ac03d3b-2532-57c0-b9b4-500023e08f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293241} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dfdbeb0-5188-55a0-995a-50f79130fd12" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294263} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bad2a675-9325-5f9e-98e8-a2644c41a27d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bad2a675-9325-5f9e-98e8-a2644c41a27d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293659} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5869a77-39c9-5092-ba56-1653742daea3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3bdc99cf-7f58-58bc-8d19-759a3c38b4ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-276786" :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $8,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=8,\\,\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},-4\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294876 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8687607-80ff-5873-a6e5-4e7b5b0a8602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8687607-80ff-5873-a6e5-4e7b5b0a8602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293711} :answer-field/id #uuid "687ed8c9-f3bb-5447-b65d-0661ab582ddb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294295} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f01dce6-e066-51e9-8e04-c9e737fc1a0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f01dce6-e066-51e9-8e04-c9e737fc1a0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293562} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1e1cb57-225d-5cba-8410-44dfe2c60e0f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bfa9ea2c-dfab-54c2-ba47-4e82fd54912f" :question/math-academy-id "q-276791" :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $12,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${60}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=12,\\,\\theta ={60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(6,6\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76b9b4bb-41ee-5123-9c9a-81ee28b29a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76b9b4bb-41ee-5123-9c9a-81ee28b29a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293419} :answer-field/id #uuid "62f664e1-3397-5fc8-8997-d85966d501de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294281} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e8a5a4c-630b-516e-b8e0-968782ed3ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e8a5a4c-630b-516e-b8e0-968782ed3ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293182} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ff41a97-f9b0-5355-aeb2-b3b9a775f59b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a88a0577-f66a-514f-9dd3-d71688a75644" :question/math-academy-id "q-276798" :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $3,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${240}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=3,\\,\\theta ={240}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({240}^{∘}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({240}^{∘}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294879 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2708c61-f2d1-53ee-a4ea-fb782ceca8db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2708c61-f2d1-53ee-a4ea-fb782ceca8db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293822} :answer-field/id #uuid "da981865-cfbd-52c1-9bab-b92faa7bdbfb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294696} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89b9cc0e-222c-5712-817e-705a58a6e766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89b9cc0e-222c-5712-817e-705a58a6e766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293467} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef892571-43d2-5d92-9083-6ce12a147796" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "60b828e5-8a1d-5f25-a3d7-f526a8ab5604" :question/math-academy-id "q-276803" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(\\frac{r}{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(\\sqrt{3},1).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{1}{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52ddb059-0d05-51a1-bd95-f4bc61d11fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52ddb059-0d05-51a1-bd95-f4bc61d11fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293267} :answer-field/id #uuid "34283d07-159f-5091-9a01-99f05e0ae58a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294110} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4492581-9f4a-54f6-8d56-efd1c2556ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4492581-9f4a-54f6-8d56-efd1c2556ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293754} :answer-field/id #uuid "a643542b-f211-57cf-9379-abb105ab8ff5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "18267ca6-6217-5e13-9301-7563b567900e" :question/math-academy-id "q-276807" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},2).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot 4,\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=(8,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-4\\sqrt{3},-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0c17d61-8b20-57f7-bc5b-640a6ec8d53b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0c17d61-8b20-57f7-bc5b-640a6ec8d53b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293866} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a127bc7-9c4c-53d5-928b-fac60ba066e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294364} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e6d10ef-69b9-50c0-90b4-1995316eadf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e6d10ef-69b9-50c0-90b4-1995316eadf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292997} :answer-field/id #uuid "826392ad-052e-559d-8fa4-5b8ae6ee49d9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8de1e1e5-b9e0-5943-ba28-afbdac6dbd1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-276838" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(5,0).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{0}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the first quadrant, we have $\\theta ={\\theta }_{R}=0.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(5,0+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(5,\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294889 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9601aa2e-abda-5507-ad25-c4cf0d606ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9601aa2e-abda-5507-ad25-c4cf0d606ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293522} :answer-field/id #uuid "13aa7ac9-c9db-5cb2-a8e4-6b8e1ab477fb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293993} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c520d556-f1f8-5b44-ba0f-87c690254537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c520d556-f1f8-5b44-ba0f-87c690254537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293697} :answer-field/id #uuid "422e7132-f763-5853-af40-ea2fb905c278" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3c0494bb-9b54-5cfd-9186-bd801e2eaf2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-277012" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d931b377a8d62bf3b56672a0f556c0581e8400c5b1c7b1f783f08e4297342855.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4,{240}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={210}^{∘}.$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4,{240}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=4.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${240}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={240}^{∘}-{210}^{∘}={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4,{30}^{∘})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e3ea9aa-2309-5688-8b5e-dd148f86cfd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e3ea9aa-2309-5688-8b5e-dd148f86cfd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293482} :answer-field/id #uuid "e142ea0a-9b5f-51f6-80c8-47978bf6ecdf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294719} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9b12f13-f42a-5b68-a20b-fb1c035b77fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9b12f13-f42a-5b68-a20b-fb1c035b77fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293714} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2b0d2fd-d64a-5114-8b1b-0b8b6064a336" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fb90e8f9-7bb2-5ebe-9317-d50976c6167d" :question/math-academy-id "q-277027" :question/problem "![](images/db/dbca0b9bd96eb68b7f874df74848bb887037a7de2ed7613bc848c40f78feff4f.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(10,{60}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={60}^{∘}.$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(10,{60}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=10.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $10,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $10.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counter-clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${60}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={60}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}={120}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(10,{120}^{∘})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,5\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8dfee2-0dcc-51dd-8359-792e8b7dcf0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324094} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc33d3f-43b1-5036-bee7-64c9f48bbe57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324353} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8acf08-5493-5784-b668-42814866416a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324861} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2836f8-1ba6-5faa-ae75-030fa6b7030c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{5},-3\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325163} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2f2323-45b4-58c8-8c06-7d06b792ddfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5\\sqrt{3},-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d8dfee2-0dcc-51dd-8359-792e8b7dcf0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324094} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2ba43e9-48cb-5399-8b07-ea56e49d1700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "351973f9-e651-502b-90b1-ba68a5d4bedc" :question/math-academy-id "q-40804" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $({r}^{2},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n((\\sqrt{10})^{2},\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=(10,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,-5\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326884 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1de63e38-80fb-54c4-9a7c-5a96600b508f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},4\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324222} {:answer/id #uuid "23d41aa2-c3a0-5270-abba-3a1f3207c3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324266} {:answer/id #uuid "2e4dd8b9-9d94-588f-a6d0-7d91017750d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324340} {:answer/id #uuid "81916b05-53d5-5cfe-b7c6-b0642b2c06ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8\\sqrt{3},8\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325056} {:answer/id #uuid "82355246-8fcd-5520-a5e5-83b3cb9b05c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8\\sqrt{2},8\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23d41aa2-c3a0-5270-abba-3a1f3207c3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324266} :answer-field/id #uuid "3036c726-f5d4-51ab-a7e4-77d619c6072b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "417c4b2b-c91e-563b-b92c-41495ea7b2b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-40826" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(8,\\theta +\\pi ).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-1}{-1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(8,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+\\pi )=(8,\\frac{9\\pi }{4})=(8,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19aefefa-5028-5a51-9e4a-e7a5f85a0ecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348661} {:answer/id #uuid "31c265f3-9b60-519e-8c42-a2074afe1991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348888} {:answer/id #uuid "61e897c5-e855-5435-b9a3-3ce1fe3df228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{9}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349343} {:answer/id #uuid "6bdd275f-98a8-5d3a-be25-406316b4b4e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349442} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ea7143-6d50-55a0-8054-cb45eeb37658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{9}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bdd275f-98a8-5d3a-be25-406316b4b4e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349442} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce1ae29-1a77-542a-a894-431caec80306" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e4c53f6c-deac-537d-ad18-d2272f3b99d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-81221" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e9d2238ca77532c3adda8d35f94061d108c3e15432ce48fdc33885ddf4f4e48.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(5,\\frac{7\\pi }{6})$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}),$ we must have $r=5.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $5,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $5.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(5,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a38cdd4-2914-5fbf-af5a-f896fca82487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{11}{2},-4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348534} {:answer/id #uuid "46388f94-0b9a-5819-926f-46042d3247f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,-4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349085} {:answer/id #uuid "46e780ae-e427-5db3-9e7b-87fd979da562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349091} {:answer/id #uuid "de7003e9-ed40-536d-a5eb-03ffaab61707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350392} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f16d9b-438b-5abd-801a-9c54b0392c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-4\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de7003e9-ed40-536d-a5eb-03ffaab61707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350392} :answer-field/id #uuid "07393c80-442e-58d3-986a-670d473a0e30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ebfe6ff0-a2a2-5246-ac85-4546ec479cc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-81224" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a615f7a80b81f89fccd8c82bf65d65e3a4a65958ec8a6fbc746ae68749949a87.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(10,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ and $m∠AOB=\\pi ,$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(10,\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we must have $r=10.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $10,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $10.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(10,\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,-5\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040aa995-2a95-5978-ae2e-5bf3e64df54b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351604} {:answer/id #uuid "327eef0b-6d33-56b7-b7fa-65647e6064b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352034} {:answer/id #uuid "8813249a-4b08-5911-ab6e-9dfd026fab0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352759} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f4b322-8686-56b2-9216-36cb838b3f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353432} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf46c88-e2c1-5c74-915f-85de76f4b18a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},8\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6f4b322-8686-56b2-9216-36cb838b3f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353432} :answer-field/id #uuid "496d734d-db4b-581d-ac63-001e27aa3e49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ab581aa-df37-556d-9193-036f42c477b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-8516" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $8,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${150}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=8,\\,\\theta ={150}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡({150}^{∘}) \\\\  & =8\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡({150}^{∘}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-4\\sqrt{3},4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08efb97a-0845-52d8-bdec-7bd31306c42b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351646} {:answer/id #uuid "230bc612-57e0-56e6-8eea-f9e3d483e43e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{2},3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351882} {:answer/id #uuid "338ca3df-505f-596c-a48d-5be76e527d0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352044} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa18674-53ae-5117-b249-67885b2aced3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},-3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352155} {:answer/id #uuid "4896ba6b-ce49-594f-aaf5-6386e658c01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "230bc612-57e0-56e6-8eea-f9e3d483e43e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{2},3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351882} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1be8990-390c-5a17-b09b-6ffc2b3112fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f70a1b51-73cd-5475-aadd-7e2e3bee491f" :question/math-academy-id "q-8519" :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(6,{45}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate od $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\ x & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\ y & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(3\\sqrt{2},3\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d257c92-35f5-5542-8460-7eedd2b1d612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352271} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfbf2f2-dbb2-5231-a8e3-5c7f27e6aec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352400} {:answer/id #uuid "66bc6397-ad23-5f9d-bbbd-4c19753bdac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352476} {:answer/id #uuid "7d900d5b-7506-5283-a33a-77e549a766d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{3},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352669} {:answer/id #uuid "af70cc0a-2339-58cb-ad6d-c2731efb201f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,3\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dfbf2f2-dbb2-5231-a8e3-5c7f27e6aec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352400} :answer-field/id #uuid "d717a7d4-1e96-542f-89a3-19928481d9b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a9b63e65-54e5-5242-a627-cab8a091bffb" :question/math-academy-id "q-86619" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(\\frac{r}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$  Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(3\\sqrt{2},3\\sqrt{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(3\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18+18} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(\\frac{6}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=(3,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535347 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "512f8089-92f8-5ea3-b9a5-7abb978d3b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535366} {:answer/id #uuid "86d506d9-bc5f-51aa-be8f-e3abef1c740a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535378} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2d81cb-c461-5f7b-8450-71a4228b9f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535393} {:answer/id #uuid "cf496e30-dd7f-59a1-b4ad-60477a502b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535394} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf77d6c-88df-5387-9154-85de6f2f6acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86d506d9-bc5f-51aa-be8f-e3abef1c740a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535378} :answer-field/id #uuid "3687de83-0f94-52d2-bb3d-db934e50e31c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0056086d-94bc-5226-9d21-585b229e6c3d" :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},{315}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a1df247-ef18-50b0-b291-d725530036be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535349} {:answer/id #uuid "2fd15eb8-1a20-5672-b35e-3baa4e9182a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535356} {:answer/id #uuid "507a57a0-b238-5c42-8a90-6a656100ff44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535365} {:answer/id #uuid "7c2fc122-0829-5452-a1a6-e7a827dae481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535375} {:answer/id #uuid "ecba5057-60ea-5479-a544-a85f670c91a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c2fc122-0829-5452-a1a6-e7a827dae481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535375} :answer-field/id #uuid "91300014-ef09-5db9-a23d-e1b17823da8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2369bcf6-10b1-57c2-a3aa-fa5b4cd63d5d" :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(6\\sqrt{2},{45}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\ x & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\ y & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(6,6).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d380b4d-cbdb-5c3d-b4f5-79f4d3f30612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d380b4d-cbdb-5c3d-b4f5-79f4d3f30612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535371} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b1e34e9-281e-5525-a988-3e0183aed79a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535351} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebc38c67-0f2c-55bc-8ac3-870b4de8cdd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :