eratorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\ x & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\ y & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\sqrt{3},3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49ba955d-0687-5313-8a94-1e8d43146764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535363} {:answer/id #uuid "5383f70f-b2db-58bf-be9a-991190a79f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535368} {:answer/id #uuid "95b14a76-aada-5d75-9c60-9bc381e42b1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535382} {:answer/id #uuid "b4221dca-faf4-5364-b247-a200dbba83c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535388} {:answer/id #uuid "ebad5aa5-4ceb-50fd-960a-5ea2aeef6720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49ba955d-0687-5313-8a94-1e8d43146764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535363} :answer-field/id #uuid "51ea380c-f23e-5e51-bd1d-f8d38228dbb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d7f1330f-7c93-5695-838c-983c4cfdaa3e" :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(3,{120}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =3\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =3\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544784 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c14f59e-67b0-5401-9f45-fbeeee924851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c14f59e-67b0-5401-9f45-fbeeee924851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544250} :answer-field/id #uuid "01f795ef-1bd6-53d0-93e4-2f6075fc9d94" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544650} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f16dc6-ec6f-552f-87c4-2a50bc7836ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sin^2\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12f16dc6-ec6f-552f-87c4-2a50bc7836ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sin^2\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544128} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4daa789-6251-5dc2-bff5-c7b29f5e7178" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "04c21b11-d6eb-5e2f-9a68-02bdca097cfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-133225" :question/problem "Let $x(\\theta )$ and $y(\\theta )$ represent the $x$- and $y$-coordinates of a point on the given curve. Write expressions for $x(\\theta )$ and $y(\\theta )$ in terms of $\\theta .$\n\n$x(\\theta )\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$y(\\theta )\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use \n\n> $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ and $\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$\n\nwhere $x,$ $y,$ and $r$ are functions of $\\theta .$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we get \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\ny=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "394e6e13-81f4-56a2-910a-dedf04a04292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "394e6e13-81f4-56a2-910a-dedf04a04292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550131} :answer-field/id #uuid "86a1a259-b391-5a29-8850-bdded74725d7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550156} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25037821-3854-562c-994e-f296a336d4d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25037821-3854-562c-994e-f296a336d4d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550128} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e034bc3-d723-558a-a6dd-0121b631b793" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "09b619c0-b536-5243-a608-187bb7fdbae4" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{\\pi}{3})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(1,0)$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot(1),\\frac{\\pi}{3})=(2,\\frac{\\pi}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(1,\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce7d179e-f508-591e-acc5-c6afaa82a765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce7d179e-f508-591e-acc5-c6afaa82a765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550165} :answer-field/id #uuid "25429db6-9731-5cad-93b2-3769a934d1b3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550129} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56185f3f-fc8b-55b9-9a8b-a974adfba2df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56185f3f-fc8b-55b9-9a8b-a974adfba2df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550137} :answer-field/id #uuid "496f73d6-8ab7-51ed-9199-483eb4b51393" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "160d6d55-a68a-5cd0-a8b9-c87c2fd8df95" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{\\pi}{6})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(1,0)$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot(1),\\frac{\\pi}{6})=(2,\\frac{\\pi}{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\sqrt{3},1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fb777fb-61ce-52be-bae8-b3c80108daf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fb777fb-61ce-52be-bae8-b3c80108daf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550141} :answer-field/id #uuid "64db8c78-ed3e-59c4-85d7-44111136c2f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550143} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5693ed90-3135-59e7-85d8-eee8e7d665c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5693ed90-3135-59e7-85d8-eee8e7d665c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550138} :answer-field/id #uuid "67693190-4101-5007-a37d-f3b8bec0edf9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "20264f6c-2c36-5db7-805a-4dc03a3191d1" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{2\\pi}{3})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(1,0)$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot(1),\\frac{2\\pi}{3})=(2,\\frac{2\\pi}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-1,\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c886efd-dc0a-54b5-98ce-ffe60d60ea44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550132} {:answer/id #uuid "6d0a4ea8-0d3c-5aa7-85bb-e50f5507f3f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550147} {:answer/id #uuid "705bcf0b-7428-5217-a7a6-a7982cd196dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550148} {:answer/id #uuid "84a75aad-a020-52d9-b21a-a5cab8307ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550155} {:answer/id #uuid "fac864e5-68ed-527c-bcb5-5a626ad820e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "705bcf0b-7428-5217-a7a6-a7982cd196dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550148} :answer-field/id #uuid "4514c27d-7de8-5fbe-af5b-39af5b0ac966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "587a5aa6-010b-5d5f-889e-d7627ae67d9d" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(4, θ)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2, θ - \\frac{π}{4})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(\\sqrt{8},-\\sqrt{8})$." :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\\\   & =\\operatorname{arctan}(1) \\\\   & =\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we have $\\theta=\\frac{7\\pi}{4}.$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2,\\frac{7\\pi}{4}+\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right))=(2,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot(0) \\\\   & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot(-1) \\\\   & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(0,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550150 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "235af48b-73d5-5181-bbf4-d811e601a21f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8\\sqrt{3},8\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550127} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf6ceee-b036-5c1f-921c-657626d22fac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(16\\sqrt{3},16\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550140} {:answer/id #uuid "6aba6e21-3c77-50a0-8331-1576457e11a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(16\\sqrt{2},16\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550146} {:answer/id #uuid "74fafcb0-63e1-558c-b040-1dbfd5369cba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550152} {:answer/id #uuid "962db716-882a-5ad6-8307-52bf8800f28e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8\\sqrt{2},8\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "962db716-882a-5ad6-8307-52bf8800f28e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8\\sqrt{2},8\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550157} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e92e34c-2e8c-5081-aa49-e66c673495c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "73990ad1-cfa6-5a8e-93b3-fc46b89fc10a" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{8},\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(16,\\theta +\\pi ).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-2}{-2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(16,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+\\pi )=(16,\\frac{9\\pi }{4})=(16,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(8\\sqrt{2},8\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65f169b7-0532-51cd-8478-f1a94bd41ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550144} {:answer/id #uuid "4b659e00-e3b7-4117-96f7-91fee90794e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65f169b7-0532-51cd-8478-f1a94bd41ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550144} :answer-field/id #uuid "72486232-f6d3-5b62-b9a2-3e7e4f12cadb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550149} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bbdaaf9-dc0c-5d8d-8232-0e7af48e54c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bbdaaf9-dc0c-5d8d-8232-0e7af48e54c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550124} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6d5fcdd-fca0-5966-aad3-6df23b58197c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7423cb94-dc90-5a23-868c-cd90a0dda4e8" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{5\\pi}{6})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(1,0)$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot(1),\\frac{5\\pi}{6})=(2,\\frac{5\\pi}{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\sqrt{3},1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be90888d-34e7-51c7-b007-7c1341e8a537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be90888d-34e7-51c7-b007-7c1341e8a537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550160} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1b43544-5038-5a80-abd2-bcf94d3add9b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550159} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30