 {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564651} {:answer/id #uuid "509c16f1-1ef5-4b9e-9aa7-fdfbda1e3a70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fae41de2-30dc-52c2-b767-4cd2fca0ebfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564651} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb4bb037-4458-5443-9885-b84764763734" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f379e31b-1455-531c-aca1-32e394f270e2" :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $1,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${45}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=1,\\,\\theta ={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e53c384-7350-51a2-a23d-c081708089f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e53c384-7350-51a2-a23d-c081708089f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601137} :answer-field/id #uuid "22b52e4e-6036-5ab3-842d-afa54ac20f83" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601338} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "068bbf83-1334-5b55-943c-6ce23d036e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "068bbf83-1334-5b55-943c-6ce23d036e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600971} :answer-field/id #uuid "22c608ba-62df-5095-b06b-26bf9c3c560c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1a60b8c5-59b6-50b9-9fed-628bf05c5f84" :question/math-academy-id "q-275952" :question/problem "![](images/ee/ee6332c301811952b6212cf9a551e41cb17b153a887407de29fdded040cf1a9a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}.$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),$ we must have $r=2\\sqrt{2}.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $2\\sqrt{2},$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta$-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{7\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(2,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0966efff-178c-5034-90c0-c88a3d3028f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600973} {:answer/id #uuid "5da099da-162a-5ea8-93d6-f4569c7b60eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601098} {:answer/id #uuid "adf5d864-282b-543b-938c-ba55715b3f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601203} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d61183-a806-5a69-bc1d-48c7ed98bf17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601277} {:answer/id #uuid "e88c7919-6c20-57e1-92ed-e0c6cf747176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5da099da-162a-5ea8-93d6-f4569c7b60eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601098} :answer-field/id #uuid "e501fce5-7f01-5d06-82cb-b95ebc2e8309" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "34703524-66bb-5912-b698-6c1e0fbc88e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-17122" :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(1,\\pi ).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\ x & =1(-1) \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\ y & =1\\cdot 0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-1,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ed5c54-5a93-5446-b877-db6c83cbe430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16ed5c54-5a93-5446-b877-db6c83cbe430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600993} :answer-field/id #uuid "98ce9edf-2fbd-5445-a453-835742b1c5c5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601396} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f462e7b-eb5c-5581-9556-892caef04e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f462e7b-eb5c-5581-9556-892caef04e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601026} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3103400-d3a0-5387-9c29-4a981ddf8ce4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "50cff3e4-68ed-58a5-bee3-b698059ae4df" :question/math-academy-id "q-277008" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6aee74fb4f0ede5e0c2aca0a068723b49af7acf029ac6be1a73b5de552b7e550.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(6,\\frac{11\\pi }{6})$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(6,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}),$ we must have $r=6.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $6,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $6.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{11\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta$-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(6,\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(3,-3\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "231687a7-3027-514f-91e3-4c5291d75b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601014} {:answer/id #uuid "3194ba33-dae3-5361-bfb5-e0d310da68d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601030} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd2010b-59fa-58c4-b502-376766e7463d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601049} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d31e46-d5c2-540f-be09-2a098a27c5d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{2},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601233} {:answer/id #uuid "edc00b5f-d0ed-527e-aa88-9d09e06d34a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3194ba33-dae3-5361-bfb5-e0d310da68d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601030} :answer-field/id #uuid "2452d964-9e46-5a30-8d02-bcc7f15cf985" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "927ecc33-9d43-5e31-a5b5-3120dd0d0d04" :question/math-academy-id "q-86621" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\theta -\\frac{\\pi }{12}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(1,\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the first quadrant, we have $\\theta ={\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{12})=(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a3bbd56-8e29-56f4-88c6-88ad7c601b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2},5\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601169} {:answer/id #uuid "ab149898-8b7e-5eef-a25d-6af563b88c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601198} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b84207-badc-5658-9172-d92486e65665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601261} {:answer/id #uuid "d4cfa1d9-acdf-5c43-a5e6-e6a1db68b4f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-5\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601264} {:answer/id #uuid "dea3a1e7-bf04-5468-a2d7-1fedabde0121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,5\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab149898-8b7e-5eef-a25d-6af563b88c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601198} :answer-field/id #uuid "12cb2bdb-8b50-5ecc-940a-f5f4a25d9ada" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c8aac56f-2bf8-5ea3-a6d1-b31fe28b87af" :question/math-academy-id "q-8517" :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(10,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\ x & =10\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ x & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\ y & =10\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ y & =5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-5,5\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bf15d37-92bf-5435-9936-19540a61a704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601005} {:answer/id #uuid "1f87042c-830f-50bf-9441-add4e80363f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{3},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601008} {:answer/id #uuid "9d181f75-f2c9-5c78-99ef-530d85de09b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\sqrt{3},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601177} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e840d7-28c3-534b-baa3-e3d52c3903d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601260} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c006c4-2f49-5e69-9c78-3332dd60ff5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d181f75-f2c9-5c78-99ef-530d85de09b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\sqrt{3},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601177} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2367487-3a83-5ee8-9d52-df658938ce3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cfbc385e-2e49-5d1a-bf7a-213296f283bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-81218" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(\\frac{r}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$  Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-4,-4\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-4\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(\\frac{8}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-2\\sqrt{3},2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66a9985b-0d93-51b2-8db2-dcfb0205c982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66a9985b-0d93-51b2-8db2-dcfb0205c982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601106} :answer-field/id #uuid "647ba955-9e18-55fb-8ea2-c26cd0590368" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601373} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62cfe5bc-affe-58ef-b557-2ce8322a1610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62cfe5bc-affe-58ef-b557-2ce8322a1610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601104} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e6a2d4e-654c-53a8-9806-4de1f7af318c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d5630f3b-13f1-584d-87df-effb4a0c3640" :question/math-academy-id "q-276782" :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $5,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\pi$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=5,\\,\\theta =\\pi .\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-5,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604035 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3aacebb9-3633-54ea-af39-bd631b9b4a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603388} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb51142-55c6-5ab6-945a-e4bcac1789f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603455} {:answer/id #uuid "793cab6b-d60c-51bb-bc07-9e76536448b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603518} {:answer/id #uuid "8de8cbec-fb13-5919-a1f8-a4e5dbb178fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603574} {:answer/id #uuid "b4b7bccc-f563-5698-b59d-624f9875ebba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5\\sqrt{2},-5\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aacebb9-3633-54ea-af39-bd631b9b4a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603388} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ac6b221-8661-5dcf-aa7d-9013f4a7262c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "704bcb07-f5a9-504f-b384-7134e574b668" :question/math-academy-id "q-86625" :question/problem "![](images/23/2345e527962c82c89c2689f17ee061ce2a6691ec58b21fff1890bed106ad5b5a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(5,\\pi )$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\pi ),$ we must have $r=5.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $5,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $5.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\pi ,$ the $\\theta$-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(5,\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe2997a-1d76-5ecd-a83c-f33b44b509d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fe2997a-1d76-5ecd-a83c-f33b44b509d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603290} :answer-field/id #uuid "52b882ba-7bfd-5d78-b1e9-611bf71f5e44" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603875} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a4e7434-68dd-5b06-814b-b625fce37ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a4e7434-68dd-5b06-814b-b625fce37ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603386} :answer-field/id #uuid "86322074-c6c5-53af-b991-669e654761fd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "88ed896e-de35-51be-8b01-2f69db5ceea4" :question/math-academy-id "q-275951" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\theta -\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-1,1).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{-1}\\right)| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)| \\\\  & =|-\\frac{\\pi }{4}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the second quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(0,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186630925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7413b259-7e18-5be8-bb45-c0232b96e7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186630600} {:answer/id #uuid "834ef774-bc2a-5c47-b967-cba23ae86f88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{2},3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186630623} {:answer/id #uuid "a1009b16-3c6d-5443-af76-239ae2f303db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186630676} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa3d9fb-dd98-5920-8c0d-378751fc6999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186630717} {:answer/id #uuid "c67eb172-9632-5cae-ab54-e02240efdf7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186630725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfa3d9fb-dd98-5920-8c0d-378751fc6999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186630717} :answer-field/id #uuid "7605a696-e80d-5376-9b6a-93c8b2b49e76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186630858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "333e7e60-81e3-55e4-867b-13b1cdc34646" :question/math-academy-id "q-120504" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(6,\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(3,\\theta -\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we have $\\theta =2\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(3,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=(3,\\pi ).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-3,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41055ac7-fdbf-575b-82f1-e2a834613816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721859} {:answer/id #uuid "4a44376e-0c3c-5d37-8fd2-b713f1dd28ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721860} {:answer/id #uuid "84fa57fb-a948-57c6-8aef-39837b08f817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721864} {:answer/id #uuid "9a98a2fa-80e0-54f2-bf88-bcd68752aa22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721867} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a3dc78-9dd7-5f3a-af35-64042bf1a3c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a98a2fa-80e0-54f2-bf88-bcd68752aa22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721867} :answer-field/id #uuid "81d07ada-a8b4-55b1-9925-ee6f84f72094" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "353cf584-808b-530b-86d4-e0918d9e97cd" :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $\\left(3,\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)$. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, use\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe angle $\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$ lies in the second quadrant and has reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$. Hence cosine is negative and sine is positive:\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\qquad\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst calculate the $x$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=3\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)\\\\\n &=3\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen calculate the $y$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=3\\sin\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)\\\\\n &=3\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}\\\\\n &=\\frac{3}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates are $\\left(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$. The negative $x$ and positive $y$ confirm the second quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ae004d-38a0-5c50-81d7-9e0f0285f38f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721855} {:answer/id #uuid "7644528e-109a-5f1d-aa21-fae42a7522a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\sqrt{3},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721861} {:answer/id #uuid "9905511f-dbe6-512d-a2d4-00918bb3a94a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721866} {:answer/id #uuid "9acd9fd0-afd5-546e-9f86-ab37967a2f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721868} {:answer/id #uuid "a2c2a46c-14f3-5c0a-9e5d-8cea88a807b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9905511f-dbe6-512d-a2d4-00918bb3a94a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721866} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf1fddd2-8ce2-5405-89dd-11a20174577e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8043b0ac-0a10-5e97-957e-d555cd373e68" :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $\\left(4,\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)$. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, use\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe right triangle shows why cosine gives the horizontal component and sine gives the vertical component.\n\n![](images/20/205b3684042b0ab0261a4d56800cd89a10a7816f8022befcf6c664337f834380.png)\n\nHere $r=4$ and $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$. First calculate the $x$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=4\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\\\\n &=4\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\\\\n &=2\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen calculate the $y$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=4\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\\\\n &=4\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}\\\\\n &=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth coordinates are positive, as expected in the first quadrant. Therefore, the Cartesian coordinates are $\\left(2\\sqrt{3},2\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "141a52ea-efe0-5fa3-989d-85b656aa4a04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "141a52ea-efe0-5fa3-989d-85b656aa4a04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721856} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c2c8deb-c510-5e95-9034-4a03b1ddb3a7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721857} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db774d92-73f9-57a9-9e94-60ee50597842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db774d92-73f9-57a9-9e94-60ee50597842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721873} :answer-field/id #uuid "93f5c24e-f0ae-5ac9-a06e-8e0c7b0531b8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ab27e4c8-0620-540c-9f5a-41d839c0c6f0" :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $\\left(2\\sqrt{3}k,\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)$, where $k$ is a positive real number. Find its exact Cartesian coordinates in terms of $k$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates are\n\n$(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Use the same conversion formulas, keeping the radius in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $k>0$, the radius $2\\sqrt{3}k$ is positive. The angle $\\frac{4\\pi}{3}$ lies in the third quadrant and has reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$, so\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2},\\qquad\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst calculate the $x$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=2\\sqrt{3}k\\cos\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\\n &=2\\sqrt{3}k\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=-\\sqrt{3}k.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen calculate the $y$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=2\\sqrt{3}k\\sin\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\\n &=2\\sqrt{3}k\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=-3k.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates are $\\left(-\\sqrt{3}k,-3k\\right)$. Both coordinates are negative for every allowed $k$, as expected in the third quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "052d5f34-1e0e-5282-b101-978370b35877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721902} {:answer/id #uuid "34f7983a-0b99-5fcc-bc7c-08b8f063d660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721905} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4547b3-1115-5508-824b-a097c7c9b334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{3},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721909} {:answer/id #uuid "70378463-ab96-5005-adeb-aa55b830c2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721911} {:answer/id #uuid "8c36e586-e05d-5e06-a182-1966758f4666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34f7983a-0b99-5fcc-bc7c-08b8f063d660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721905} :answer-field/id #uuid "390e939e-843c-5bca-8d6d-d39b67418d59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0dc03ed6-e002-5e8e-9e05-0abe7881f116" :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $2$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $30^{\\circ}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P$?\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$, with $\\cos 30^{\\circ}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\sin 30^{\\circ}=\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nThe diagram shows how the two coordinates relate to the radius and angle.\n\n![A first-quadrant right triangle with radius r, angle theta, horizontal leg x equals r cosine theta, and vertical leg y equals r sine theta.](images/20/205b3684042b0ab0261a4d56800cd89a10a7816f8022befcf6c664337f834380.png)" :question/worked-solution "The description gives the polar quantities $r=2$ and $\\theta=30^{\\circ}$. The horizontal coordinate uses cosine and the vertical coordinate uses sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=r\\cos\\theta=2\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\sqrt{3},\\\\\ny&=r\\sin\\theta=2\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth coordinates are positive because $P$ is in the first quadrant. In the order $(x,y)$, the Cartesian coordinates are $(\\sqrt{3},1)$. The distance is $\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^2+1^2}=2$, as required."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "909a1056-1360-5e99-9667-88bc5fc89d8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "909a1056-1360-5e99-9667-88bc5fc89d8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721913} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeb6bedf-3d80-5345-ad1c-bd936ffabd49" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721915} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31f3df21-fcb1-5219-a6d4-36cbc17ffd92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31f3df21-fcb1-5219-a6d4-36cbc17ffd92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721904} :answer-field/id #uuid "eda00b62-2d32-578d-a30c-4fbf082a7581" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9d35c697-8cb4-5969-add2-78dba432d7b6" :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $2\\sqrt{3}$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$.\n\n$(x,y)=(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The description gives $r=2\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\theta=\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$. The angle lies in the second quadrant with reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$, so\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\qquad\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nConvert each coordinate, including the radical in the radius:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=r\\cos\\theta=(2\\sqrt{3})\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=-\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{3}=-3,\\\\\ny&=r\\sin\\theta=(2\\sqrt{3})\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the Cartesian coordinates are $(-3,\\sqrt{3})$. The signs agree with the second quadrant, and $x^2+y^2=9+3=12=(2\\sqrt{3})^2$. Multiplying the two square roots in the $x$-coordinate gives $3$, rather than leaving a single factor of $\\sqrt{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a56347d-f156-54d8-becf-9401900b9460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721907} {:answer/id #uuid "6cd1942f-17d9-5262-8107-8b248abb2d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721910} {:answer/id #uuid "b35421ac-4249-5bfd-9205-469cc554fe25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721916} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d9a5e6-582b-57e3-9d26-f4754f595458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721917} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a9bd3d-1dfb-541d-8c7e-97084768fc31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4a9bd3d-1dfb-541d-8c7e-97084768fc31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721919} :answer-field/id #uuid "57ceb10b-8310-5402-b986-496426b093a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d5c1b464-c4d7-57f5-af9a-340665b0f961" :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $3$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $210^{\\circ}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. What are the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$?" :question/worked-solution "The description gives $r=3$ and $\\theta=210^{\\circ}$. This angle is in the third quadrant with reference angle $30^{\\circ}$, so both cosine and sine are negative:\n\n$$\n\\cos 210^{\\circ}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\qquad\n\\sin 210^{\\circ}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=3\\cos 210^{\\circ}=3\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\\\\ny&=3\\sin 210^{\\circ}=3\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates are $\\left(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$. As a check, $x^2+y^2=\\frac{27}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}=9=r^2$, and both coordinates have the third-quadrant signs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18dc8ed5-0eb6-5265-8f96-706d4de24a4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721921} {:answer/id #uuid "80836a02-9e1d-58a7-95b1-6e0ac8e8eccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721929} {:answer/id #uuid "80f26a90-5e26-5e52-b24f-84f92d44444e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721930} {:answer/id #uuid "c930c7cd-1e21-5052-8582-78a62393007a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721935} {:answer/id #uuid "cd08f9a5-6b96-599b-9fc7-16b95f50d978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80f26a90-5e26-5e52-b24f-84f92d44444e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721930} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0a7816e-e285-5174-afab-861180b3d7f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4262c717-975c-5907-a4c0-2f112dda35e8" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$, where $r>0$, and Cartesian coordinates $(2,0)$. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{\\pi}{3})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nUse $x=R\\cos\\phi$ and $y=R\\sin\\phi$ for a point with polar coordinates $(R,\\phi)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $A=(2,0)$ lies on the positive $x$-axis, its distance from the origin is $r=2$. Thus the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2r,\\tfrac{\\pi}{3})=(4,\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe lesson's right triangle shows why the Cartesian coordinates of a point are its radius times the cosine and sine of its angle:\n\n![](images/20/205b3684042b0ab0261a4d56800cd89a10a7816f8022befcf6c664337f834380.png)\n\nUsing the radius $4$ and angle $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ of $B$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=4\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=4\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}=2,\\\\\ny_B&=4\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=4\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(2,2\\sqrt{3})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "354f4645-ce5d-5125-a619-6f7af51ff5de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721922} {:answer/id #uuid "5c704991-320f-5bda-9cb5-02e76d853f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721926} {:answer/id #uuid "6784e06f-23df-53f4-b0e4-8b398e8d6d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721927} {:answer/id #uuid "93fbe734-9a08-5bd2-b375-ffb58a8eb0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721932} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9d4691-e5d1-544f-b3ee-943ba315c03e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6784e06f-23df-53f4-b0e4-8b398e8d6d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721927} :answer-field/id #uuid "36058a43-ce58-5e65-b557-e8894698601b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "eb1691f4-2c26-5ec5-bcce-bef44dcb6e7a" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\theta)$ and Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt{3},1)$. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(3,\\theta+\\frac{\\pi}{3})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$." :question/worked-solution "First, find the reference angle of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\alpha=\\arctan\\left(\\left|\\frac{y_A}{x_A}\\right|\\right)=\\arctan\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi}{6}.\n$$\n\nSince $x_A<0$ and $y_A>0$, the point $A$ lies in the second quadrant. We may take\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\pi-\\alpha=\\frac{5\\pi}{6}.\n$$\n\nThe angle of $B$ is therefore\n\n$$\n\\phi_B=\\theta+\\frac{\\pi}{3}=\\frac{5\\pi}{6}+\\frac{2\\pi}{6}=\\frac{7\\pi}{6}.\n$$\n\nAny coterminal choice of $\\theta$ gives the same Cartesian point. The polar coordinates of $B$ are $(3,\\frac{7\\pi}{6})$. Now convert using $x_B=3\\cos\\phi_B$ and $y_B=3\\sin\\phi_B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=3\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=3\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\\\\ny_B&=3\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=3\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e0b4c99-02e6-5ee8-9770-deb5fbe3546a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e0b4c99-02e6-5ee8-9770-deb5fbe3546a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721931} :answer-field/id #uuid "721c0c4a-7e23-5976-ac01-3b51e61bc51c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721928} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4760f36b-168c-543c-b09e-b2f8a65c3a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4760f36b-168c-543c-b09e-b2f8a65c3a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721925} :answer-field/id #uuid "aadb8ad8-c463-55cc-b83a-d92fdc80bb3f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f84b9c04-ea6a-54fe-bfad-5eced44c6532" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{\\pi}{4})$, where $r>0$, and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(\\frac{r}{3},\\frac{7\\pi}{6})$. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "First, recover the radius of $A$ from its Cartesian coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr&=\\sqrt{x_A^2+y_A^2}\\\\\n &=\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^2+(\\sqrt{2})^2}=\\sqrt{4}=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{7\\pi}{6})$. We use $x_B=R_B\\cos\\phi_B$ and $y_B=R_B\\sin\\phi_B$ with the radius and angle of $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=\\frac{2}{3}\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\\\\ny_B&=\\frac{2}{3}\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{1}{3})$. Both coordinates are negative, as expected for an angle in the third quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722034 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0054d665-01d1-5ea1-9378-ab85faf0bd1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,6\\sqrt3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722032} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee808d4-af2a-5df8-b35f-8ad95da3eacb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,3\\sqrt3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722036} {:answer/id #uuid "69173e59-210d-56b0-843f-432ad48b4be2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722037} {:answer/id #uuid "7b85f5e1-8025-5b2d-9ab3-528b4d97435b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\sqrt2,3\\sqrt2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722039} {:answer/id #uuid "ad4833bf-7003-5f64-9747-6a612f706499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\sqrt3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ee808d4-af2a-5df8-b35f-8ad95da3eacb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,3\\sqrt3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722036} :answer-field/id #uuid "581b4f00-97f4-5cce-943c-012853cdb1af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a428970-dc91-57ae-867f-6c5959a4a2b1" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef353618883e0fa610a179cb3103e9a59cf7bff8f6147317cf65f45bb868b6e6.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin $O$. The polar coordinates of $A$ are $(6,150^\\circ)$ and $\\angle AOB=30^\\circ$. Use the marked arc and the positions of $A$ and $B$ to determine whether the angle of $B$ is greater or less than the angle of $A$.\n\nWhat are the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ and $B$ lie on the same circle, $B$ also has radius $6$. The marked arc runs clockwise from $A$ toward $B$, so subtract the included angle:\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=150^\\circ-30^\\circ=120^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThus the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(6,120^\\circ)$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=6\\cos120^\\circ=6\\left(-\\frac12\\right)=-3,\\\\\ny&=6\\sin120^\\circ=6\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)=3\\sqrt3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $B=(-3,3\\sqrt3)$. The signs agree with its position in quadrant II. The angle $30^\\circ$ is the turn between the two radii, not the polar angle of $B$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722049 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72003291-d8f9-5026-980c-b1b498b459fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,3\\sqrt3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722038} {:answer/id #uuid "9320f210-f816-5328-8a2b-9a35bc42d8e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\sqrt2,3\\sqrt2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722042} {:answer/id #uuid "aa42cf96-a8a4-5de3-a40e-29d64a7a6b69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,6\\sqrt3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722043} {:answer/id #uuid "b4cc1548-7364-579c-b0d5-7317dfbd4e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac12,\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722045} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c31346-feb7-57af-8085-a8681e5ad1bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\sqrt3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72003291-d8f9-5026-980c-b1b498b459fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,3\\sqrt3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722038} :answer-field/id #uuid "ceec72b7-ab47-512b-9d33-c6c93506f3f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d8837aec-8346-54f8-a096-0a9eb1f9dac7" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1ca3b41e82afa7c9a6dd513ecc900fc41ec08432027f4fa692836299bf5c3540.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(6,30^\\circ)$ and $\\angle AOB=30^\\circ$.\n\nMoving from $A$ to $B$ along the marked arc is counterclockwise, so add the marked angle to the angle of $A$. Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$ to find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nWhich ordered pair is correct?" :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so the radius for $B$ is $r=6$. The marked counterclockwise turn increases the polar angle:\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=30^\\circ+30^\\circ=60^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(6,60^\\circ)$. Convert each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=6\\cos60^\\circ=6\\left(\\frac12\\right)=3,\\\\\ny&=6\\sin60^\\circ=6\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)=3\\sqrt3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(3,3\\sqrt3)$. Both coordinates are positive, as the diagram requires."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722050 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be2a6f55-5e77-5553-8eeb-628d61f2f3bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be2a6f55-5e77-5553-8eeb-628d61f2f3bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722046} :answer-field/id #uuid "08e05cf7-837f-57bd-b4c5-f91fec5c5acd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722033} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fcb5740-06e6-569c-90c2-47cbbfad75f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fcb5740-06e6-569c-90c2-47cbbfad75f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722040} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fa6598d-903f-5b20-a1c0-a995802f84b1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ee62f03f-0cea-511b-8e0c-0abfe290e4e4" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c450bcb722f49ed4300043f18bc5f8b61c44d25b91312974df4ef8884d9dd8d4.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin $O$. If the polar coordinates of $A$ are $\\left(6,\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=\\frac{7\\pi}{12}$, determine the exact Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "The two points lie on the same circle, so the radius for $B$ is $6$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is clockwise. Subtract the marked angle, using a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=\\frac{5\\pi}{6}-\\frac{7\\pi}{12}\n=\\frac{10\\pi}{12}-\\frac{7\\pi}{12}\n=\\frac{3\\pi}{12}=\\frac\\pi4.\n$$\n\nThe polar coordinates of $B$ are therefore $\\left(6,\\frac\\pi4\\right)$. Convert using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=6\\cos\\left(\\frac\\pi4\\right)=6\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\right)=3\\sqrt2,\\\\\ny&=6\\sin\\left(\\frac\\pi4\\right)=6\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\right)=3\\sqrt2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $B=(3\\sqrt2,3\\sqrt2)$. Its equal positive coordinates agree with the point on the first-quadrant diagonal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7cd0e27-36a8-5d99-a9fb-36ec474df23a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7cd0e27-36a8-5d99-a9fb-36ec474df23a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722269} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ca08921-b3b0-58b7-b2a9-3ad78255f07c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722265} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88498f4b-1173-579b-b9b9-1b34756b6f45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88498f4b-1173-579b-b9b9-1b34756b6f45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722262} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2d987e8-1a14-5576-a516-dc2dc166699f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "786a4c08-1e4b-5744-a7df-59615a923660" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\theta)$ and Cartesian coordinates $(0,-\\sqrt{2})$. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\theta-\\frac{\\pi}{4})$. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "The point $A$ lies on the negative $y$-axis, so we may take $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$.\n\nApply the signed angular offset:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\phi_B&=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}-\\frac{\\pi}{4}=\\frac{5\\pi}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(2,\\frac{5\\pi}{4})$. Convert each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=2\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right)=2\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=-\\sqrt{2} \\\\\ny_B&=2\\sin\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right)=2\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=-\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc6f1a58-5a5c-5f21-ae1c-32200fa5f430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc6f1a58-5a5c-5f21-ae1c-32200fa5f430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722270} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c324edd-be6e-5ba1-bb77-585ca097ea4e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722253} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7b67aa0-719c-56dc-9707-8274cf1a1f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7b67aa0-719c-56dc-9707-8274cf1a1f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722273} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeb71845-bf4f-564d-9590-ec8c8721d214" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7c1240a8-8c2d-5714-90d4-591fbd172f14" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{5\\pi}{3})$ and Cartesian coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{6})$. The radius $r$ is positive. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{7\\pi}{6})$. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "First, recover the positive radius of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr&=\\sqrt{x_A^2+y_A^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^2+(-\\sqrt{6})^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the radial relationship:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_B&=2\\cdot(2\\sqrt{2})=4\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi}{6})$. Convert each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=4\\sqrt{2}\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=4\\sqrt{2}\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=-2\\sqrt{6} \\\\\ny_B&=4\\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=4\\sqrt{2}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-2\\sqrt{6},-2\\sqrt{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e19af3f0-3dda-5c68-bcaa-a3517e941d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e19af3f0-3dda-5c68-bcaa-a3517e941d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722271} :answer-field/id #uuid "08e0815f-88c3-52c6-9127-868390879814" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722249} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67c7f912-4e88-5ca8-8684-c08c2e9cdff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67c7f912-4e88-5ca8-8684-c08c2e9cdff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722256} :answer-field/id #uuid "894f7550-df6f-55a2-aae7-b6a13d42f378" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "90ee7654-bfe0-5ac6-8d2d-9babd3746dce" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(6,\\theta)$ and Cartesian coordinates $(0,6)$. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(3,\\theta+\\frac{\\pi}{4})$. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "The point $A$ lies on the positive $y$-axis, so we may take $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\nApply the signed angular offset:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\phi_B&=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+\\frac{\\pi}{4}=\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(3,\\frac{3\\pi}{4})$. Convert each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=3\\cos\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)=3\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\\ny_B&=3\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)=3\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "332adade-bed7-5d79-9394-ec3c2b13fa76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "332adade-bed7-5d79-9394-ec3c2b13fa76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722252} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ce1a4df-0e6f-536f-91d3-6a3052462204" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722254} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "707a795f-2229-58f3-9dc5-347fd74b061a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "707a795f-2229-58f3-9dc5-347fd74b061a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722258} :answer-field/id #uuid "6db39753-96e1-5610-89f0-fbd0fad54a19" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b0d248b9-2238-5a27-85b7-408b469ae92e" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{4\\pi}{3})$ and Cartesian coordinates $(-3,-3\\sqrt{3})$. The radius $r$ is positive. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(r^{2},\\frac{3\\pi}{4})$. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "First, recover the positive radius of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr&=\\sqrt{x_A^2+y_A^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{(-3)^2+(-3\\sqrt{3})^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{36}=6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the radial relationship:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_B&=(6)^2=36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(36,\\frac{3\\pi}{4})$. Convert each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=36\\cos\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)=36\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=-18\\sqrt{2} \\\\\ny_B&=36\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)=36\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=18\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-18\\sqrt{2},18\\sqrt{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83880857-9538-54b4-aede-58b1f1e4a758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83880857-9538-54b4-aede-58b1f1e4a758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722261} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a6283db-5ae5-5c6c-b601-810953907a31" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722251} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15319a44-8550-5918-9074-d06efaff0ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15319a44-8550-5918-9074-d06efaff0ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722250} :answer-field/id #uuid "5446c58b-eb64-5ab9-8cc0-f15a69b691a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b4b11be6-bf1b-5525-9fda-436f7f9aa080" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{4\\pi}{3})$ and Cartesian coordinates $(-1,-\\sqrt{3})$. The radius $r$ is positive. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(\\frac{r}{3},\\pi)$. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "First, recover the positive radius of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr&=\\sqrt{x_A^2+y_A^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{(-1)^2+(-\\sqrt{3})^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{4}=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the radial relationship:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_B&=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot(2)=\\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},\\pi)$. Convert each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=\\frac{2}{3}\\cos\\left(\\pi\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}\\left(-1\\right)=-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\\ny_B&=\\frac{2}{3}\\sin\\left(\\pi\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}\\left(0\\right)=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{2}{3},0)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2a7c93d-b74e-571c-ae6e-780a47aad9c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2a7c93d-b74e-571c-ae6e-780a47aad9c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722302} :answer-field/id #uuid "194f4826-7077-54e5-8849-2abe94a19542" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722279} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8cf27de-9b94-5cdf-8375-75a43530969e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8cf27de-9b94-5cdf-8375-75a43530969e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722304} :answer-field/id #uuid "80969cf2-0643-55ab-9449-9b6e216d6ed8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1386f7d0-60a4-5ef3-a8e0-8a4a9a079685" :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $7$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $270^{\\circ}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$.\n\n$(x,y)=(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The description gives $r=7$ and $\\theta=270^{\\circ}$. The point is on the negative $y$-axis. Thus $\\cos(270^{\\circ})=0$ and $\\sin(270^{\\circ})=-1$.\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$. Calculate the horizontal coordinate first, then the vertical coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=r\\cos\\theta\\\\\n&=(7)\\cos(270^{\\circ})\\\\\n&=(7)(0)\\\\\n&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=r\\sin\\theta\\\\\n&=(7)\\sin(270^{\\circ})\\\\\n&=(7)(-1)\\\\\n&=-7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian coordinates are $(0,-7)$. As a check, $x^2+y^2=0+49=49=r^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "197a271a-b76c-5504-86e7-3f49d0398436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722280} {:answer/id #uuid "36e69d7f-3e32-5a28-b2aa-813e391af69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{3}{4},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722282} {:answer/id #uuid "8cce678e-c102-5b76-829c-9fa8050021bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{1}{2},- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722296} {:answer/id #uuid "a794e539-ea61-522c-a388-dfac8d653207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722298} {:answer/id #uuid "a99aa229-1756-5c45-b565-b200e0d3ce39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,- 3 \\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "197a271a-b76c-5504-86e7-3f49d0398436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722280} :answer-field/id #uuid "319d266e-3dd3-5af7-91ea-e7ddec41a8d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "437a97e2-4575-5c68-bc87-7a2927b210d7" :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $3$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "The description gives $r=3$ and $\\theta=\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}$. The angle is in quadrant $3$ with reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. Thus $\\cos\\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)=- \\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\sin\\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$. Calculate the horizontal coordinate first, then the vertical coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=r\\cos\\theta\\\\\n&=(3)\\cos\\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\\n&=(3)\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=r\\sin\\theta\\\\\n&=(3)\\sin\\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\\n&=(3)\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian coordinates are $(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2})$. As a check, $x^2+y^2=\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{4}=9=r^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d2b7729-5417-5844-90bf-2d11c0239c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d2b7729-5417-5844-90bf-2d11c0239c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722288} :answer-field/id #uuid "54545cd3-2159-5429-bbf7-56b03db3f679" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722290} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "437d6b6a-33df-55f3-8965-90ab771044fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "437d6b6a-33df-55f3-8965-90ab771044fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722285} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6feb54c-d46c-55ff-88fe-fd025c5bceb7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "448b16cb-5709-5e8b-a344-76b65d4955c7" :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $3$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$.\n\n$(x,y)=(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The description gives $r=3$ and $\\theta=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}$. The angle is in quadrant $2$ with reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$. Thus $\\cos\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and $\\sin\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$. Calculate the horizontal coordinate first, then the vertical coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=r\\cos\\theta\\\\\n&=(3)\\cos\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)\\\\\n&=(3)\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=r\\sin\\theta\\\\\n&=(3)\\sin\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)\\\\\n&=(3)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian coordinates are $(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2})$. As a check, $x^2+y^2=\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}=9=r^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025b4cd8-cb52-5559-a7a7-9afb3a0cae1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722275} {:answer/id #uuid "108bbd60-821c-5efb-bc28-2da38c62ed16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722276} {:answer/id #uuid "8b832af9-27f7-5eec-912c-46fcc8bfbe4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722295} {:answer/id #uuid "952e7f5c-f55b-59f0-af2d-97a11c4fcd0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{16},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{16})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722297} {:answer/id #uuid "abddd75b-9c7f-5d38-ad0c-f927f3918abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b832af9-27f7-5eec-912c-46fcc8bfbe4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722295} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f2b1955-808b-5387-9b70-8fa901803b62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4641e0f6-47b8-546f-adac-8949e16b137b" :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $\\frac{1}{4}$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $225^{\\circ}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "The description gives $r=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\theta=225^{\\circ}$. The angle is in quadrant $3$ with reference angle $45^{\\circ}$. Thus $\\cos(225^{\\circ})=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and $\\sin(225^{\\circ})=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$. Calculate the horizontal coordinate first, then the vertical coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=r\\cos\\theta\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cos(225^{\\circ})\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=r\\sin\\theta\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\sin(225^{\\circ})\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian coordinates are $(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8})$. As a check, $x^2+y^2=\\frac{1}{32}+\\frac{1}{32}=\\frac{1}{16}=r^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15fd0b07-8629-528b-acf2-4c453cb45d9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722278} {:answer/id #uuid "52572a14-ad04-5725-8744-d5f87a2144d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722289} {:answer/id #uuid "55e14521-0880-504f-93b7-2a92e43d6038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8},\\frac{1}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722291} {:answer/id #uuid "72030818-9dab-53fa-8f4f-fe9e554b6b67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\sqrt{3},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722292} {:answer/id #uuid "dce31149-a8d9-5a49-b746-1a5d85e9d298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},- \\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52572a14-ad04-5725-8744-d5f87a2144d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722289} :answer-field/id #uuid "74640ca2-eb0a-5c84-9a7c-a6821e5b3b9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "faa5d0c6-91cf-5871-b4c9-00908ab21c68" :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $\\frac{1}{2}$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $150^{\\circ}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "The description gives $r=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\theta=150^{\\circ}$. The angle is in quadrant $2$ with reference angle $30^{\\circ}$. Thus $\\cos(150^{\\circ})=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\sin(150^{\\circ})=\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$. Calculate the horizontal coordinate first, then the vertical coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=r\\cos\\theta\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cos(150^{\\circ})\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=r\\sin\\theta\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\sin(150^{\\circ})\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian coordinates are $(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\frac{1}{4})$. As a check, $x^2+y^2=\\frac{3}{16}+\\frac{1}{16}=\\frac{1}{4}=r^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1acf6b72-2c89-5fd9-80dc-857c7111e264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{11}{2},- \\frac{11 \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722336} {:answer/id #uuid "7de5b324-1a0f-50ba-886b-7730b4658c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{11 \\sqrt{2}}{2},- \\frac{11 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722350} {:answer/id #uuid "cee43005-2853-5e1f-9a93-f3cde911dc0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{11}{2},- \\frac{11 \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722362} {:answer/id #uuid "dd54c9cc-4fde-5037-ab02-937310945ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{11 \\sqrt{2}}{2},- \\frac{11 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722364} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a30707-dd61-5e24-9f82-cae24699378c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{11 \\sqrt{3}}{4},- \\frac{11}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7de5b324-1a0f-50ba-886b-7730b4658c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{11 \\sqrt{2}}{2},- \\frac{11 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722350} :answer-field/id #uuid "765de4aa-9b68-5006-8265-7009a634c78d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6c1a7344-97ea-522d-b42a-364fafbd8792" :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $\\left(11,\\frac{7 \\pi}{4}\\right)$. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, use\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst calculate the $x$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=11\\cos\\left(\\frac{7 \\pi}{4}\\right)\\\\\n &=11\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=\\frac{11 \\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen calculate the $y$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=11\\sin\\left(\\frac{7 \\pi}{4}\\right)\\\\\n &=11\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=- \\frac{11 \\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates are $\\left(\\frac{11 \\sqrt{2}}{2},- \\frac{11 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722347 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac1ad1fe-4e18-5549-9b77-3b9a9f9a043e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{7}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722359} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d16c78-4278-5ca7-80b6-df2473c58353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(7 \\sqrt{2},7 \\sqrt{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722360} {:answer/id #uuid "dd459448-8537-5f62-b608-baa9b7232a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722363} {:answer/id #uuid "fc903747-346d-55a5-95ca-034749f05411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{2},- \\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722368} {:answer/id #uuid "fda3f863-6198-54f3-906c-99c4f9bf2ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{2},- \\frac{7}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fda3f863-6198-54f3-906c-99c4f9bf2ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{2},- \\frac{7}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722369} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1496f80-68d7-5de9-af77-d3f11720a43d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7535f5dd-1e51-581b-a692-b843882fdc51" :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $\\left(7,330^{\\circ}\\right)$. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, use\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst calculate the $x$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=7\\cos\\left(330^{\\circ}\\right)\\\\\n &=7\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=\\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen calculate the $y$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=7\\sin\\left(330^{\\circ}\\right)\\\\\n &=7\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=- \\frac{7}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates are $\\left(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{2},- \\frac{7}{2}\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e184113-3ad7-518a-8fe9-7a19c0f3a6b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(- 2 \\sqrt{2},- 2 \\sqrt{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722337} {:answer/id #uuid "53bbbd03-b47d-5c3e-a8d9-21cabdfa55b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(-2,- 2 \\sqrt{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722341} {:answer/id #uuid "750fe037-311f-595c-bbcb-0